Graph-based and transition-based dependency parsing are two methods used in natural language processing to understand the structure of sentences. Graph-based parsers, like MSTParser or McDonald's parser (2006), use a global scoring function that considers all possible parse trees for an input sentence before choosing the best one. Transition-based parsers such as MaltParser work by incrementally constructing a dependency tree and deciding which action to take at each step, typically using deterministic methods like beam search with predefined heuristics (Nivre et al., 2006).

Recent advancements propose combining the two approaches: Graph-based transition-based parsing utilizes graph data structures but employs a greedy or efficient decoding mechanism, such as beam search. This hybrid model has been shown to outperform traditional single systems on Penn Treebank (PTB) and Chinese language benchmarks like ConLL X tasks with accuracies of 92.1% for English and even higher in the case study reported by McDonald's parser, which surpassed previous best results by achieving a high accuracy rate beyond what was previously thought possible on these data sets (McDonald et al., 2005; Buchholz & Marsi, 2 employee_salary = {'John':3000, 'Sarah':4000, 'Mike':2500}
